Cut-based Electron Identification for Run3¶
Overview¶
The cut-based electron identification for Run3 provides a set of working points that use rectangular cuts on kinematic and isolation variables to select electrons. This is the standard approach for electron identification in CMS and offers excellent performance for most physics analyses.

Approval presentation

Working Points¶
The Run3 cut-based electron ID provides several working points with different signal efficiency and background rejection:

Veto: (~95%) Highest signal efficiency, moderate background rejection. Use this working point for lepton veto or counting.
Loose: (~90%) Balanced efficiency and background rejection. Use this working point when backgrounds are rather low.
Medium: (~80%) Lower signal efficiency, better background rejection. This is a good starting point for generic measurements involving W or Z.
Tight: (~70%) Lowest signal efficiency, highest background rejection. Use this working point for measurements where backgrounds are a serious problem.
Note that for measurements focusing on high energy (~1+ TeV) electrons, a dedicated HEEP ID is provided see a dedicated link on the EgammaIDRecipesRun2 twiki. HEEP ID for Run3 has not been developed yet, till then Run2 HEEP ID mentioned above can be used and expected to provide reasonable performance. In case you find worse performance using the Run2 HEEP ID till we mention a dedicated HEEP ID for Run3 please report to us.

Implementation in CMSSW¶
The cut-based electron ID is implemented in CMSSW using the VID (Video ID) framework. The recommended usage is through the official CMSSW implementation rather than manual implementation of the cuts.

Cut Variables¶
The cut-based ID uses the following variables:

σ_{iηiη}: Shower shape in η direction
|Δη|: Track-supercluster matching in η
|Δφ|: Track-supercluster matching in φ
H/E: Ratio of hadronic to electromagnetic energy
Relative isolation: Combined relative isolation
|1/E - 1/p|: Consistency between track momentum and supercluster energy
Missing hits: Number of missing hits in the tracker
Conversion rejection: Veto on photon conversions
